+\begin{standardfact}[Classical square-wave Gibbs behavior]\label{sf:gibbs_behavior}
+Let \(q(x)=\tfrac12\operatorname{sign}(\sin x)\), whose jump at zero has size one. For \(n\geq1\), its partial sum through the first \(n\) positive odd frequencies is
+\[
+Q_n(x)=\frac{2}{\pi}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\frac{\sin((2k+1)x)}{2k+1}.
+\]
+At \(x_n=\pi/(2n)\),
+\[
+x_n\longrightarrow0,\qquad Q_n(x_n)\longrightarrow\frac{\operatorname{Si}(\pi)}{\pi}
+=\frac12+G,\qquad G\approx0.089489872236,
+\]
+where \(\operatorname{Si}(z)=\int_0^z\sin(t)/t\,dt\) and \(G=\operatorname{Si}(\pi)/\pi-1/2>0\).
+Thus a nonzero overshoot persists along points approaching the jump. Scaling by a real amplitude \(a\) gives an overshoot or undershoot of magnitude \(|a|G\).
